{:edb/db-before-t 860 :edb/db-after-t 861 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534173 50 #inst "2026-10-11T17:26:41.703Z" 13194139534173 17592186045417 true] [17592186179729 1038 #edb/ref 17592186413043 13194139534173 17592186045417 true] [17592186179734 1038 #edb/ref 17592186413042 13194139534173 17592186045417 true] [17592186179858 1050 720.0 13194139534173 17592186045417 true] [17592186180007 1068 #edb/ref 17592186177711 13194139534173 17592186045417 false] [17592186180007 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413040 13194139534173 17592186045417 true] [17592186180007 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534173 17592186045417 true] [17592186180007 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to $\\displaystyle \\int_(C)(x + y)dx + (x - y)dy$, where $C$ is parametrized by $r(t) = 4ti + (4 - 4t)j$ for $t ∈ [0, 1]$?" 13194139534173 17592186045417 false] [17592186180007 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}(x+y)\\,dx+(x-y)\\,dy,$ where $C$ is parametrized by $r(t)=4t\\,i+(4-4t)\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,1]?$" 13194139534173 17592186045417 true] [17592186180007 1074 "Along the curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=4t,\\,y(t)=4-4t.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4-4t)\\,j \\\\  & =4\\,i+(-4)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(x+y)\\,dx+(x-y)\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(x+y)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+(x-y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}((x+y)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+(x-y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(4t+(4-4t))\\cdot 4+(4t-(4-4t))\\cdot (-4)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}16t+16-16t-16t+16-16t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}32-32t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =32{\\int }_{0}^{1}1-t\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534173 17592186045417 true] [17592186180013 1068 #edb/ref 17592186177747 13194139534173 17592186045417 false] [17592186180013 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413037 13194139534173 17592186045417 true] [17592186180013 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534173 17592186045417 true] [17592186180013 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to $\\displaystyle \\int_(C)\\ln (x + y)dx + (x + y)dy$, where $C$ is parametrized by $r(t) = i + tj$ for $t ∈ [0, 2]$?" 13194139534173 17592186045417 false] [17592186180013 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)\\,dx+(x+y)\\,dy,$ where $C$ is parametrized by $r(t)=i+t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2]?$" 13194139534173 17592186045417 true] [17592186180013 1074 "Along the curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=1,\\,y(t)=t.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,j \\\\  & =j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)\\,dx+(x+y)\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+(x+y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+(x+y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+t)\\cdot 0+(1+t)\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}1+t\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534173 17592186045417 true] [17592186180176 1068 #edb/ref 17592186178600 13194139534173 17592186045417 false] [17592186180176 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413036 13194139534173 17592186045417 true] [17592186180176 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534173 17592186045417 true] [17592186180176 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to $\\displaystyle \\int_(C)ydx + xdy + z^{2}dz$, where $C$ is a portion of the helix $r(t) = \\cos ti + \\sin tj + tk$ for $t ∈ [0, 2π]$?" 13194139534173 17592186045417 false] [17592186180176 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}y\\,dx+x\\,dy+{z}^{2}\\,dz,$ where $C$ is a portion of the helix $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+t\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ]?$" 13194139534173 17592186045417 true] [17592186180176 1074 "Along the curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,z(t)=t.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,k \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}y\\,dx+x\\,dy+{z}^{2}\\,dz & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+{z}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+{z}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)+{t}^{2}\\cdot 1\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t+{t}^{2}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+{t}^{2}\\,dt\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534173 17592186045417 true] [17592186180295 1068 #edb/ref 17592186179314 13194139534173 17592186045417 false] [17592186180295 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413039 13194139534173 17592186045417 true] [17592186180295 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534173 17592186045417 true] [17592186180295 1071 "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_(C) - ydx + xdy + zdz$, where $C$ is a portion of the helix $r(t) = \\cos ti + \\sin tj + tk$ for $t ∈ [0, 2π]$." 13194139534173 17592186045417 false] [17592186180295 1071 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}-y\\,dx+x\\,dy+z\\,dz,$ where $C$ is a portion of the helix $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+t\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ].$" 13194139534173 17592186045417 true] [17592186180295 1074 "Along the curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,z(t)=t.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,k \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}-y\\,dx+x\\,dy+z\\,dz & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }-y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(-y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+t\\cdot 1\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t+t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }1+t\\,dt \\\\  & =(t+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}){|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =2\\pi +\\frac{(2\\pi )^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =2\\pi (1+\\pi )\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534173 17592186045417 true] [17592186180681 1064 "Line integrals with respect to $x$ and $y$ often come in pairs. For example, \n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C (x+y) \\, \\textrm dx + \\int\\limits_C (x-y) \\, \\textrm dy.\n$$\n\nWe usually express a sum of line integrals with respect to $x$ and $y$ as a single integral. In the case above, we can write this sum as\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C (x+y) \\, \\textrm dx + (x-y) \\, \\textrm dy.\n$$\n\nLet's evaluate this integral in this case where $C$ is given by the line segment\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=\\underbrace{(6t+1)}_{x(t)} \\,\\mathbf{i} + \\underbrace{(2t+1)}_{y(t)} \\,\\mathbf{j}, \\qquad t\\in\\left[0,1\\right].\n$$\n\nAlong the path $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx = 6t+1, \\qquad y = 2t+1.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative of $\\mathbf r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6t+1)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t+1)\\,j \\\\  & =6\\,i+2\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the line integral by applying a change of variables, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}x+(x-y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(x+y)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+(x-y)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}((x+y)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+(x-y)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(6t+1+2t+1)\\cdot 6+(6t+1-(2t+1))\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(8t+2)\\cdot 6+(4t)\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}48t+12+8t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}56t+12\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={28{t}^{2}+12t|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =28+12 \\\\  & =40.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534173 17592186045417 false] [17592186180681 1064 "Line integrals with respect to $x$ and $y$ often come in pairs. For example, \n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C (x+y) \\, \\textrm dx + \\int\\limits_C (x-y) \\, \\textrm dy.\n$$\n\nWe usually express a sum of line integrals with respect to $x$ and $y$ as a single integral. In the case above, we can write this sum as\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C (x+y) \\, \\textrm dx + (x-y) \\, \\textrm dy.\n$$\n\nLet's evaluate this integral in this case where $C$ is given by the line segment\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=\\underbrace{(6t+1)}_{x(t)} \\,\\mathbf{i} + \\underbrace{(2t+1)}_{y(t)} \\,\\mathbf{j}, \\qquad t\\in\\left[0,1\\right].\n$$\n\nAlong the path $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx = 6t+1, \\qquad y = 2t+1.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative of $\\mathbf r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6t+1)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t+1)\\,j \\\\  & =6\\,i+2\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the line integral by applying a change of variables, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}x+(x-y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(x+y)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+(x-y)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}((x+y)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+(x-y)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(6t+1+2t+1)\\cdot 6+(6t+1-(2t+1))\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(8t+2)\\cdot 6+(4t)\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}48t+12+8t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}56t+12\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={28{t}^{2}+12t|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =28+12 \\\\  & =40.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534173 17592186045417 true] [17592186413006 1080 "16{\\int }_{0}^{2}2t-1\\,\\text{d}t" 13194139534173 17592186045417 true] [17592186413006 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534173 17592186045417 true] [17592186413006 1083 #uuid "0f99d644-a1ad-5b43-b8be-36588f2a8d57" 13194139534173 17592186045417 true] [17592186413007 1080 "{\\int }_{1}^{4}8{t}^{3}-8{t}^{2}+3t\\,dt" 13194139534173 17592186045417 true] [17592186413007 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534173 17592186045417 true] [17592186413007 1083 #uuid "207e611c-1715-5848-8636-a148354824ca" 13194139534173 17592186045417 true] [17592186413008 1080 "{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+t)+t+1\\,\\text{d}t" 13194139534173 17592186045417 true] [17592186413008 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534173 17592186045417 true] [17592186413008 1083 #uuid "355201fd-a87c-5acd-a773-040c3348da1d" 13194139534173 17592186045417 true] [17592186413009 1080 "60" 1